readings.c1s2:
¶
{{smaller block|Those smiling eyes ''attemp’ring'' every ray.
{{right|''Pope'', Eloisa to Abelard, l. 63.}}
Those [influences] which, in older and more normally constituted communities, modify and ''attemper'' Mammon-worship.
{{right|''The American'', IV. 65.}}}}
'''3.''' To mix in just proportion; regulate.
{{smaller block|God hath so ''attempered'' the blood and bodies of fishes.
{{right|''Ray'', Works of Creation.}}
<poem>Pure of blame,
In praise and in dispraise the same,
A man of well-''attemper’d'' frame.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Duke of Wellington.}}}}
'''4.''' To accommodate; fit or make suitable.
{{smaller block|<poem>The joyous birdes, shrouded in chearefull shade,
Their notes unto the voice ''attempred'' sweet.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., II. xii. 71.}}
Arts ''attempered'' to the lyre.
{{right|''Pope.''}}
[In all its uses nearly obsolete, ''temper'' being generally used.]}}

'''attemperament''' (a-tem′pėr-a̤-ment), ''n.'' A tempering or mixing in due proportions. Also ''attemperment.''

'''attemperance†''' (a-tem′pėr-a̤ns), ''n.'' [< ME. ''attemperaunce'', < OF. ''atemprance'', < ''atemprer'', attemper. Cf. ''temperance.''] Temperance.

'''attemperate''' (a-tem′pėr-āt), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''attemperated'', ppr. ''attemperating''. [< L. ''attemperatus'', pp. of ''attemperare'', attemper: see ''attemper.''] '''1†.''' To attemper; regulate.
{{smaller block|If any one do . . . ''attemperate'' his actions accordingly.}}

readings.c2s1:
see ''tempt''.] '''1.''' To make an effort to effect or do; endeavor to perform; undertake; essay: as, to ''attempt'' a bold flight.
{{smaller block|The wise and prudent conquer difficulties by daring to ''attempt'' them.
{{right|''Rowe.''}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Something ''attempted'', something done,
Has earned a night’s repose.</poem>
{{right|''Longfellow'', Village Blacksmith.}}}}
'''2.''' To venture upon: as, to ''attempt'' the sea.—'''3†.''' To make trial of; prove; test: as, “well-''attempted'' plate,” ''Fairfax.''—'''4†.''' To try with afflictions. ''Jer. Taylor.''—'''5†.''' To endeavor to obtain or attract.
{{smaller block|This man of thine ''attempts'' her love.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of A., i. 1.}}}}
'''6.''' To try to win or seduce; tempt; entice.
{{smaller block|He will never . . . ''attempt'' us again.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., iv. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>It made the laughter of an afternoon,
That Vivien should ''attempt'' the blameless king.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Merlin and Vivien.}}}}
'''7.''' To attack; make an effort against; assail: as, to ''attempt'' the enemy’s camp; to ''attempt'' a person’s life.
{{smaller block|<poem>Muster
Our soldiers up; we’ll stand upon our guard;
For we shall be ''attempted''.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Thierry and Theodoret, i. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|Calumny never dared to suspect her morals, or ''attempt'' her character.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', The Bee, No. 3.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn. 1.''' ''Attempt'', ''Essay'', ''Undertake'', ''Endeavor'', ''Strive'', ''Struggle'', seek, aim. The italicized words agree in expressing the beginning of a task, physical or intellectual, which is difficult and often impossible. They are arranged in the order of strength. ''Attempt'' is to try with some effort.}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller|''Essay'' is sometimes to try in order to see if a thing can be done or attained, and sometimes simply to attempt: as, “which the Egyptians ''assaying'' to do were drowned,” Heb. xi. 29. ''Undertake'' is, literally, to take a task upon one’s self, perhaps formally, and hence to go about a task with care and effort. ''Endeavor'' is to try with more earnestness, labor, or exertion. ''Strive'' is to work hard and earnestly, doing one’s best. ''Struggle'' is to tax one’s powers to the extent of fatigue, pain, or exhaustion. The first three words are more appropriate for a single effort, the other three for continuous or continual efforts.}}
{{smaller block|None are very violent against it [writing plays in verse] but those who either have not ''attempted'' it, or who have succeeded ill in their attempt.
{{right|''Dryden'', Ded. of Ess. on Dram. Poesy.}}}}
{{smaller block|Instinct led him [Tennyson] to construct his machinery before ''essaying'' to build. ''Stedman'', Vict. Poets, p. 155.}}
{{smaller block|I will . . . ''undertake'' one of Hercules’ labours.
{{right|''Shak.'', Much Ado, ii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|In what I did ''endeavour'', it is no vanity to say, I have succeeded.
{{right|''Dryden'', Annus Mirabilis.}}}}
{{smaller block|A certain truth possesses us, which we in all ways ''strive'' to utter.
{{right|''Emerson'', Clubs.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>O limed soul, that, ''struggling'' to be free,
Art more engag’d!</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, iii. 3.}}}}

'''attempt''' (a-tempt′), ''n.'' [< ''attempt'', ''v.''] '''1.''' A putting forth of effort in the performance or accomplishment of that which is difficult or uncertain; essay, trial, or endeavor; effort.
{{smaller block|<poem>The ''attempt'', and not the deed,
Confounds us.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, ii. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|By his blindness maim’d for high ''attempts''.
{{right|''Milton'', S. A., l. 1221.}}}}
'''2.''' An effort to accomplish something by force or violence; an attack or assault: as, an ''attempt'' upon one’s life.

readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|For the better defense of his highness’s loving subjects in the same shires in case of any invasion or other ''attemptate'' by foreign enemies.
{{right|''Somerset'', quoted in Dixon’s Hist. Church of Eng., xv., note.}}}}

'''attempter''' (a-temp′tėr), ''n.'' '''1.''' One who attempts, tries, or endeavors.—'''2.''' One who attacks or assails; an assailant.
{{smaller block|<poem>Against the ''attempter'' of thy Father’s throne.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. R., iv. 603.}}}}
'''3†.''' A tempter. ''Milton.''

'''attemptive''' (a-temp′tiv), ''a.'' [< ''attempt'' + ''-ive''.] Ready to attempt; enterprising; venturous. ''Daniel.''

'''attend''' (a-tend′), ''v.'' [< OF. ''atendre'', F. ''attendre'', wait, reflex. expect, = Sp. ''atender'' = Pg. ''attender'' = It. ''attendere'', < L. ''attendere'', stretch toward, give heed to, < ''ad'', to, + ''tendere'', stretch: see ''tend'', and cf. ''attempt''.] I. ''trans.'' '''1.''' To fix the mind upon; listen to; have regard or pay heed to; consider. [Archaic. See II., 1.]
{{smaller block|The diligent pilot . . . doth not ''attend'' the unskilful words of a passenger.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney.''}}
<poem>The crow doth sing as sweetly as the lark
When neither is ''attended''. {{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., v. 1.}}

Their hunger thus appeased, their care ''attends''
The doubtful fortune of their absent friends.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden.''}}}}
'''2.''' To accompany or be present with, as a companion, minister, or servant, or for the fulfilment of any duty; wait upon.
{{smaller block|<poem>The fift had charge sick persons to ''attend''.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., I. x. 41.}}
<poem>Let one ''attend'' him with a silver bason.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., Ind., i.}}
<poem>You shall have men and horses to ''attend'' you,</poem>}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|And money in your purse.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Rule a Wife, ii. 3.}}}}
'''3.''' To be present at or in for purposes of duty, business, curiosity, pleasure, etc.: as, to ''attend'' a meeting.—'''4.''' To accompany or follow in immediate sequence, especially with a causal connection: said of things: as, a cold ''attended'' with fever; a measure ''attended'' with bad results.
{{smaller block|A correspondent revolution in things will ''attend'' the influx of the spirit.
{{right|''Emerson'', Nature.}}}}
'''5†.''' To wait or stay for; expect, as a person or an event.
{{smaller block|Thy intercepter, . . . bloody as the hunter, ''attends'' thee at the orchard end.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., iii. 4.}}
<poem>The trumpets, next the gate, in order plac’d,
''Attend'' the sign to sound the martial blast.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Pal. and Arc., l. 1741.}}}}
'''6†.''' To be in store for; await.
{{smaller block|The state that ''attends'' all men after this.
{{right|''Locke.''}}
<poem>One fate ''attends'' us, and one common grave.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', tr. of Lucretius, iii. 304.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To give attention; pay regard or heed: followed by ''to:'' as, my son, ''attend to'' my words.
{{smaller block|''Attend to'' the voice of my supplications.
{{right|Ps. lxxxvi. 6.}}
It will be sufficient for me if I discover many Beauties or Imperfections which others have not ''attended to.''
{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 262.}}}}
'''2.''' To be present, in pursuance of duty, business, or pleasure; especially, act as an attendant: absolutely, or with ''on'' or ''upon'', or ''at:'' as, who ''attends'' here? to ''attend upon'' a committee; to ''attend at'' such a church. Hence—'''3.''' To fix the mind in worship: with ''on'' or ''upon.''

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|That ye may ''attend upon'' the Lord without distraction.
{{right|1 Cor. vii. 35.}}}}
'''4.''' To be consequent; wait: with ''on'' or ''upon''.
{{smaller block|It is good that a certain portion of disgrace should constantly ''attend on'' certain bad actions.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Moore’s Byron.}}}}
'''5†.''' To stay; wait; delay.
{{smaller block|<poem>For this perfection she must yet ''attend'',
Till to her Maker she espoused be.</poem>
{{right|''Sir J. Davies'', Immortal. of Soul.}}}}

'''attend†''' (a̤-tend′), ''n.'' [< ''attend'', ''v.''] Attendance.
{{smaller block|<poem>Stars have made your fortunes climb so high,
To give ''attend'' on Rasni’s excellence.</poem>
{{right|''Greene and Lodge'', Looking Glass for Lond. and Eng.}}}}

'''attendance''' (a̤-ten′dans), ''n.'' [< ME. ''attendaunce'', < OF. ''atendance'' = Pr. ''atendensa'', < ML. ''attendentia'', < L. ''attenden(t-)s'', ppr. of ''attendere'': see ''attend'' and ''-ance''.] '''1.''' The act of attending or attending on. {{smaller|(''a'') The act of waiting on or serving; the state of being present for purposes of duty, business, pleasure, etc.; service; ministry.}}
{{smaller block|No man gave ''attendance'' at the altar. {{right|Heb. vii. 13.}}
Lindamira, a lady whose . . . constant ''attendance'' at church three times a day had utterly defeated many malicious attacks upon her reputation.
{{right|''Fielding'', Joseph Andrews.}}
The other, after many years’ ''attendance'' upon the duke, was now one of the bed-chamber to the prince.
{{right|''Clarendon.''}}}}
{{smaller|(''b''†) Attention; regard; careful application of mind.}}
{{smaller block|Give ''attendance'' to reading. {{right|1 Tim. iv. 13.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c''†) A waiting on, as in expectation.}}
